@* A lifecycle state, rendered as a colour-coded pill.

   Colour alone never carries the meaning - the status text is always present. A pill that says
   only "green" is unreadable to the ~8% of men with a colour vision deficiency, and invisible
   to a screen reader.

   Two additions beyond the colour:

     * A tooltip in plain words, so hovering a status you have never seen before answers the
       question instead of sending you to the documentation.
     * A slow pulsing dot on the states that are still moving. It is the difference between a
       list that looks like a photograph and one that looks alive - and it is information, not
       decoration: the dot marks exactly the rows something outside this screen is about to
       change. Terminal states do not pulse, because nothing is going to happen to them. *@

<span class="pill pill-@Status.ToString().ToLowerInvariant() @(OrderStatusText.IsMoving(Status) ? "pill-live" : null)"
      title="@OrderStatusText.Kid(Status)">@Status</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>The state to render.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public OrderStatus Status { get; set; }
}
